Hi there,

If you find yourself assigning an output (in this case, "output$map <- renderPlot(...)") inside of an observe, that's usually a good indication that you've gone astray. (The same goes for creating an observer inside of an output's renderPlot function.)

When you assign output$map <- renderPlot(...), that's not saying "Update the output 'map' with this logic, right now." Instead it's saying, "The output 'map' should always reflect this logic; make sure it stays up-to-date." So in this case you probably want something like this:

s_options <- buildOptions()

shinyServer(function(input, output, session) {

  updateSelectInput(session,"ONE", choices = s_options)

  output$map <- renderPlot({
    firstChoice <- input$ONE
    secondChoice <- input$TWO
    if (is.null(input$TWO))  # or maybe this should be a check for "", not sure
      return(NULL)
    otherValue <- doSomething(input$ONE, input$TWO)
    plotSomething(otherValue)
  })

  # The only reason we need an observe is to update the choices for TWO.
  observe({
    updateSelectInput(session, "TWO",
      choices = getChoicesForTwo(input$ONE))
  })
})

Note that you don't need to tell Shiny when output$map needs to be updated. It automatically infers that output$map depends on input$ONE and input$TWO, so whenever either of those changes it will automatically re-execute.

