Hi Nick,

Thanks for starting this discussion; I'm interested to see how others in the community feel about these concerns. I'll throw out my 2 cents.

First, the port 3838 issue is merely one of configuration. Shiny Server Open Source is easily capable of running on whatever port(s) your organization feel is appropriate (including 80). (http://rstudio.github.io/shiny-server/os/latest/#default-configuration)

While we happen not to use Apache or PHP, we are based on similar open source and battle-tested frameworks. Our hope with the advent of our installers <http://www.rstudio.com/shiny/server/install-opensource> is that we could take a lot of the uncertainty out of the process of setting up Shiny Server. Just a couple of commands will get you a fully configured Shiny Server instance (though you certainly have the power to change directories, ports, and a myriad of other options as you see fit).

I completely understand that many organizations are wary of new technologies (I've been there!). Our goal with Shiny Server Pro is to alleviate some of those concerns and make R feel a bit more accessible -- precisely for audiences like IT managers. Check out the screenshots (by clicking on the small cameras in the comparison table) on the Shiny Server page <http://www.rstudio.com/shiny/server/>. The idea is to present IT groups who may have never heard of R -- let alone Shiny Server -- with a familiar dashboard that they can understand. This way, without ever having to touch R, an administrator can get in and inspect/manage/kill the various applications and processes running on the server.

If you're in a spot to setup and administer the server yourself and you're not in need of any of the Enterprise features offered in Pro, I'd say put the Open Source edition on port 80 and, as far as your IT group is concerned, you're just running another web application. If you're looking for your IT group to be comfortable administrating the server or if you're in need of the expanded features in Pro, then you can go that route. We've had multiple sales calls with IT groups who were initially hesitant about "this new R thing" and walked away feeling comfortable with the product. We'd be happy to have someone run through the product with your IT team, if you'd like. Just reach out at sa...@rstudio.com

Best,
Jeff

